# Revisions listed here are skipped by `git blame`.
#
# Only add commits that are purely mechanical (formatters, licence headers,
# whitespace) and provably contain no behavioural change. Never add a commit
# that mixes formatting with real edits -- blame would then hide real authorship.
#
# Enable locally with:
#   git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs
#
# GitHub applies this file automatically in its blame view.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ACTION REQUIRED AFTER MERGING THE REPO-WIDE FORMATTING PR
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This repository merges pull requests with *squash* only (merge commits and
# rebase merges are disabled). Squashing rewrites every commit in a PR into a
# single new commit, so the per-commit SHA of the "apply isort + black across
# the repository" commit does not exist on `main` and cannot be listed here in
# advance.
#
# Git silently ignores entries in this file that do not resolve to a real
# commit, so a stale placeholder SHA would fail open: blame would keep showing
# the formatting commit as the last author of every reformatted line, with no
# error to indicate the entry was dead.
#
# So, immediately after this PR is squash-merged, a maintainer must:
#   1. Copy the squashed commit SHA from `main` (`git log -1 --format=%H`).
#   2. Append it below with a one-line description.
#   3. Push that one-line change directly to `main` (or via a follow-up PR).
#
# Until that is done, `git blame` on the reformatted lines will attribute them
# to the formatting commit rather than to their original authors.

# <SHA>  style: apply isort + black across the repository
